{meta: title 1460 Elder Street}
{meta: artist Red Sovine}
{meta: composer Bun Wilson and Doc Richardson}
{meta: x_source classic-country}
{meta: x_source_file 1460elderstreetlyricschords.pro}
{meta: x_enriched 2025-12-26}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[C]I was waiting for a [F]ship to [C]Vietnam
[G7]And me being the good time loving guy I am
[C]I was looking for some [F]female [C]company
[F]I met a girl [C]alone and lonesome [G7]just like [C]me
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[F]We had coffee at a waterfront [C]cafe
[G7]She said her husband was a soldier far away
[C]And she talked about [F]her long nights all [C]alone
[F]And the way she's [C]fought temptation [G7]since he's [C]gone
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
[F]1460 Elder Street is where she took [C]me
[F]And I never knew that [C]love could be so [G7]sweet
[C]Then she promised me [F]that when the war was [C]over
[F]I'd have a home at [G7]1460 Elder [C]Street
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
[F]Two months later I was on patrol one [C]day
[G7]When a hand grenade fell not ten feet away
[C]I watched as one brave man [F]gave all that he could [C]give
[F]He fell across [C]it gave his life [G7]that I might [C]live
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 5}
[F]I found a crumpled tear stained letter in his [C]coat
[G7]And I read the Dear John Letter his wife wrote
[C]He had nothing left for [F]livin' for I [C]guess
[F]I thought I die [C]when I [G7]saw her return [C]address
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 6}
[F]1460 Elder Street still drives me [C]crazy
[F]And sometimes at night [C]we find it hard to [G7]sleep
[C]Could it be the ones [F]who killed him now are [C]sharing
[F]Our private hell [G7]at 1460 Elder [C]Street
{end_of_verse}

{meta: title 16th Avenue}
{meta: artist Lacy J. Dalton}
{meta: composer Thom Schuyler}
{meta: x_source classic-country}
{meta: x_source_file 16thavenuelyricschords.pro}
{meta: x_enriched 2025-12-26}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[G]From the corners of the country from the [D7]cities and the [G]farms
With [Em]years and years of [C]living tucked up [G]underneath their [D7]arms
They [Em]walked away from [C]everything just to see [G]a dream come [C]true
So God [G]bless the boys who [Em]make the noise on [C]16th [G][D7]Av-enue
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
With a million dollar spirit and an old [D7]flat top [G]guitar
They [Em]drive to town with [C]all they own in a [G]hundred dollar [D7]car
Cause [Em]one time someone [C]told them about a [G]friend of a friend they [C]knew
Who [G]owns you know a [Em]studio on [C]16th [G][D7]Av-enue
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
Now some are born to money they never [D7]had to say [G]survive
And [Em]others swing a [C]nine pound hammer [G]just to stay [D7]alive
There's [Em]cowboys drunks and [C]Christians mostly [G]white and black and [C]blue
They've [G]all dialed the phone [Em]direct to home from [C]16th [G][D7]Av-enue
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
Ah but then one night in some empty room [D7]where no curtains ever [G]hung
Like a [Em]miracle some golden [C]words roll off [G]of someone's [D7]tongue
And [Em]after years of being [C]nothing they're all [G]looking right at [C]you
And [G]there for awhile [Em]they'll go in style on [C]16th [G][D7]Av-enue
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 5}
Hey it looks so uneventful so quiet [D7]and [G]discreet
But a [Em]lot of lives were [C]changed there on that [G]little one-way [D7]street
Cause [Em]they walked away from [C]everything just to see [G]a dream come [C]true
So [G]God bless the boys who [Em]make the noise on [C]16th [G][D7]Av-enue
{end_of_verse}

